  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to tackle the minimization of the Erd\u0151s overlap integral through a multi-resolution Fourier-informed coordinate descent (FCDC) approach. This combines three key innovations:\n\n- **Resolution-based Adaptation**: Evaluate solutions at multiple frequency scales (coarser to finer) to escape local optima.\n- **Fourier-domain Constraints**: Use explicit low-frequency components to guide the optimization and enforce the integral constraint.\n- **Adaptive Barrier Optimization**: Employ a logarithmic barrier method to handle inequality constraints while leveraginG low-frequency features.\n\nGiven the non-convex nature of the problem, the method directly solves a convex relaxation in the Fourier domain with sparsity-promoting regularization. Key justifications:\n\n- Fourier analysis reveals the overlap integral structure and identifies low-frequency content essential for optimal solutions.\n- Multi-resolution analysis ensures solutions at both coarse and fine scales.\n- The barrier method smoothly handles constraints while the adaptivity preserves the solution's feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nVariables:\n- Time/frequency variables: \u03be (frequency space), \u03c4 (time lag)\n- Discrete Fourier transform: \u0125 = (1/\u221an) \u03a3 h(t) e^{-i 2\u03c0\u03bet/n}\n- Solution representation: h(t) specified in time domain; \u0125 in frequency domain.\n\nConstraints:\n1. Integral constraint: T\u2080\u00b9 h(t) dt = 1\n2. Bound constraints: 0 \u2264 h(t) \u2264 1 \u2200t \u2208 [0,1]\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C\u2085(\u03be) = max_\u03c4 |\u0125(\u03be) \u0125\u0304(\u03be - \u03c4)|  (Fourier domain formulation)\n\nFourier transforms are computed via truncated FFT with n_period points, preserving boundary conditions. The optimization is performed on a computational grid.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nCore substitution algorithm:\n\nPhase A: Global Structure Discovery\n- Discretize [0,2] into N points (start with log-spaced grid from 40 to 512 points)\n- Compute initial Fourier basis functions F\u2096(t) = cos(2\u03c0kt/N) (k=0,...,floor(N/2))\n- Initialize h\u2080(t) = F\u2080(t) (low-frequency component)\n- Apply incremental resolution doubling (doubling N at each stage)\n\nPhase B: Time-Frequency Descent\nFor each resolution level:\n  - Project time-domain solution \u0125 onto a localized low-frequency space \u0125 = U\u1d40\u0125 (with U being orthgonalization matrix)\n  - Solve the barrier-regularized problem:\n    minimize_\u0125 ||\u0125||\u2081 sujet \u00e0: \n      T\u2080\u00b9 F(dot product) class = 1\n      -||log(||h||)\u00b2|| relax. \u2265 0.7\n      Adaptive barrier coefficient \u03b1 \u2208 [0.001, 0.1]\n  - Update through coordinate descent by varying frequency components\n  - Compute final solution h(t) via inverse FFT\n  - Check feasibility with project_feasible() (clamping and rescaling)\n\nPhase C:Polynomial Transformation\n- Post-process solutions through low-degree polynomial fitting to enforce strict bounds\n- Verify all solutions meet project_feasible() criteria\n- Interpolate finest-level solution to original grid\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n- **Frequency-based Initialization**: Start with sinc-like [0,1]-solutions with slowly varying amplitude\n- **Adaptive Barter Sizing**: Automatically select barrier weights based on solution curvature\n- **Frequency Boosting**: Prioritize degrees that exhibit periodic structure matching boundary conditions\n- **Core-multi Resolution**: Progressively increase frequency count from 32 to 2048 points\n- **Fourier-sight Rendition**: Tailor transformation operators to handle sharp boundary behavior\n\n5. Budget and Implementation\n\nRuntime calculated dynamically:\n\n  time_factor = 0.2 / N   # N=resolution points\n  max_time_per_level = budget_s * time_factor\n  num_levels = int(log(N)/log(2)) \n\nFor each N level:\n  CCDS_steps = min(4000, remaining_time/(0.1*N))\n  Execute FCDC with:\n    FFT_cycles = min(5, log(N)) \n    Barrier_strength = [0.01, 0.1, 0.5]\n    Prefiltering = True if N>256\n\nTerminate when:\n  Best solution's C5 < 0.38 (threshold) \n  Or elapsed time > budget_s\n  \n6. Compatibility Rules\n\n- Design to handle variable grid sizes\n- Maintain N-consistent for multi-resolution comparison\n- Verify all intermediate solutions with project_feasible()\n- Permit fallback to parent solution if all resolutions fail\n\nThe FCDC solution is intended to deliver superior C5 bounds by combining resolution-aware optimization and Fourier-domain methods.",
